# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
A(mk('facebook-disabled-appeal-rejected',
 'Facebook appeal rejected: what you can still do',
 'Facebook Appeal Rejected? What You Can Still Do',
 'Facebook rejected your appeal and the account is disabled? What a rejection means, the checks still worth making, and what not to do next.',
 'facebook', 'Appeal rejected', 'Facebook',
 'read the exact wording first, because some “rejections” are unfinished steps. Facebook says a failed appeal leaves the account permanently disabled with no second review, so the useful checks left are a hack, your Pages and ads, and your data.',
 'A rejected Facebook appeal feels final, and on Facebook’s own help page it usually is: an appeal that does not succeed leaves the account permanently disabled. Before you accept that, there are a few things worth checking, and a few moves that make everything worse.',
 [('what', 'What a rejection means',
   '<p>Facebook’s Help Center page <em>My personal Facebook account is suspended or disabled</em> says that if you don’t appeal within the time allowed, or your appeal isn’t successful, the account is permanently disabled and you won’t be able to request another review. That is the official position, and no outside party can change what Facebook offers you.</p>'
   '<p>It also helps to know what you are looking at. A suspension gives you a window to disagree. A permanently disabled account has been through that window. Facebook can also disable an account without a suspension first for severe or time-sensitive violations, so some people never see the appeal step at all.</p>'),
  ('s1', 'Step 1: Read the exact wording and the date',
   '<p>Open the email Facebook sent and log in to see the notice. Some screens that feel like a rejection are not one: an ID upload that failed, a request for more information, an appeal that is still pending, or an “account locked” checkpoint, which is a different flow. Screenshot the notice, the date and any wording about whether you can still request a review.</p>'
   '<p>If the wording says the decision can’t be reversed, treat the appeal route as closed and move to the steps below.</p>'),
  ('s2', 'Step 2: Check whether the account was hacked',
   '<p>Accounts taken over by someone else are often disabled for what the attacker posted: scam links, fake sales, messages to your friends. If your email, number or password changed before the notice, or you saw posts you did not make, go to <strong>facebook.com/hacked</strong> on a device you have used before. That flow is built for compromised accounts and can offer identity checks that the normal disabled screen does not.</p>'
   '<p>Be honest about the dates. If you never lost control, don’t describe it as a hack. A false story is easy to spot and will hurt you.</p>'),
  ('s3', 'Step 3: Secure what the account was connected to',
   '<p>A disabled profile can take other things with it. Facebook’s help pages explain that a Page with no one who has full control is deactivated, and that ad accounts where the disabled person was the only user can be disabled as well. Ask anyone who shares your Page, business portfolio or ad account to confirm they have full control, and write down which assets were linked to the profile.</p>'
   '<p>Change the password on the email address that was attached to the account, and on any other account that used the same password.</p>'),
  ('s4', 'Step 4: Save your data where you still can',
   '<p>If Facebook still lets you reach a download option from the notice screen, take it. If not, collect what you can from other places: photos in your phone’s gallery, chats on devices that are still logged in to Messenger, and contact details for customers or friends you only knew through Facebook. Tell the people who matter that you have lost access, without sharing the old login.</p>'),
  ('dont', 'What not to do',
   '<ul><li><strong>Don’t open a new account to get around the decision.</strong> Meta’s Transparency Center says it works to disable accounts created to evade earlier restrictions.</li><li><strong>Don’t resubmit the same appeal</strong> in a loop. If a review option is still open, use it once, completely.</li><li><strong>Don’t pay anyone who claims a special way in.</strong> Nobody outside Meta can reverse a final decision, and “recovery” offers aimed at disabled accounts are a common scam.</li><li><strong>Don’t hand over your password or codes</strong> to anyone offering to “try again” for you.</li></ul>'),
  ('help', 'Where an outside helper fits',
   '<p>When the self-service route has ended, there is not much a helper can honestly do, and anyone promising a result is not being straight with you. What we can do is look at the facts with you: whether the notice really is final, whether the hacked-account route applies, which Pages and ad accounts need protecting, and what to gather. We handle the process through Meta’s official channels only. Meta makes the final decision, and we tell you plainly if we don’t think it is doable.</p>')],
 [('Can I appeal a Facebook decision twice?', 'Facebook’s help page says that once an appeal is unsuccessful the account is permanently disabled and you can’t request another review. If your notice still shows a review option, use it once and complete every step.'),
  ('Does a rejected appeal mean my Pages are gone?', 'Not automatically. A Page stays active if another person has full control of it. A Page where the disabled profile was the only person with full control can be deactivated.'),
  ('Can I make a new Facebook account?', 'Meta says it works to disable accounts created to get around earlier restrictions. Read what your own notice says, and don’t create an account for the purpose of evading the decision.'),
  ('Is the account hacked or disabled?', 'If your login details changed or you saw activity you didn’t do, use facebook.com/hacked. If you only see a disabled notice, the disabled-account process applies. Some accounts are both.')],
 [FB_DISABLED, FB_HACKED, FB_RECOVERY, FB_HELP],
 [('/guides/facebook-account-disabled-appeal/', 'Facebook disabled account appeal'), ('/facebook-account-suspended-180-days/', 'Facebook suspended, 180 days'), ('/guides/hacked-facebook-account-recovery/', 'Hacked Facebook account recovery'), ('/facebook-account-disabled-appeal-help/', 'Facebook disabled appeal help')],
 ['/guides/facebook-account-disabled-appeal/', '/facebook-account-disabled-appeal-help/', '/facebook-account-suspended-180-days/'],
 'my Facebook appeal was rejected and the account is disabled. Can you take a look?', 'Facebook appeal rejected',
 ('Appeal rejected?', 'Tell us exactly what the notice says', 'Send a screenshot of the notice and the date. We will tell you honestly what is still open and what is not.'),
 ('Still unsure?', 'We’ll go through it with you', 'Message us the account name and the screen you see. We use official routes only and never ask for your password or codes.'),
 pill='Facebook appeal rejected'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(mk('facebook-account-disabled-for-no-reason',
 'Facebook account disabled for no reason? What to check',
 'Facebook Account Disabled for No Reason? What to Check',
 'Facebook disabled your account and you don’t know why? What usually triggers it, how to read the notice, and the steps to take before the deadline.',
 'facebook', 'Disabled', 'Facebook',
 'read the notice for the date, then disagree with the decision in the app before the deadline. Facebook doesn’t always name the rule, and a hack is a common hidden cause.',
 'It is common to log in, find a disabled or suspended notice, and have no idea what you did. Facebook’s notice often names a broad category rather than a specific post. Here is how to work out what probably happened and what to do about it while there is still time.',
 [('why', 'Why there may be no clear reason',
   '<p>Facebook’s Help Center says a profile can be suspended when Meta finds that the account, or activity on it, may not follow the Community Standards. The notice might point to a category, such as impersonation or unwanted contact, and not to a specific post. Automated systems also make mistakes, and Meta’s own pages acknowledge that decisions can be appealed for that reason.</p>'
   '<p>So “no reason given” rarely means “no reason exists.” It means you need to look at what was happening around the date on the notice.</p>'),
  ('causes', 'The usual triggers worth checking',
   '<ul><li><strong>Someone else used the account.</strong> A changed email, a login from a place you have never been, or posts you did not write.</li><li><strong>Name or identity.</strong> A profile name that is not the name you go by, a business, or a pet account run as a person. Facebook says misrepresenting your identity is a reason for action.</li><li><strong>A burst of activity.</strong> Many friend requests, group invitations, messages or tags in a short time, especially to people you do not know.</li><li><strong>Reports.</strong> Other people reporting posts, messages or the profile.</li><li><strong>Marketplace or ad activity.</strong> Listings, ads or a business portfolio connected to the profile.</li><li><strong>Age rules.</strong> Facebook says some accounts are suspended or disabled for a specific age range under local law.</li></ul>'),
  ('s1', 'Step 1: Capture the notice before you do anything',
   '<p>Screenshot the full notice, the date, and the number of days left to appeal. Save the email Facebook sent. If you can still reach Account Status (click your profile picture, then Help &amp; support, then Account Status), write down any strikes or restrictions on your profile and on Pages you manage.</p>'),
  ('s2', 'Step 2: Check for a hack',
   '<p>Look through the email linked to the account for messages about a changed email, a new device or a password reset. If any of those are not yours, go to facebook.com/hacked on a device you have used before and say so in any appeal. Secure your email first, so the same person cannot undo your progress.</p>'),
  ('s3', 'Step 3: Disagree with the decision',
   '<p>Log in and follow the prompts to disagree with the decision, then finish every step until you see confirmation. You may be asked to confirm a phone number, solve a security check, take a video selfie or upload ID. Use your real details and a document that matches your profile name.</p>'
   '<p>If there is room to explain, keep it short and factual: who you are, how you use the account, and that you believe the decision was a mistake. Don’t guess at a rule you may have broken and don’t paste a long template.</p>'),
  ('s4', 'Step 4: Wait without resubmitting',
   '<p>Meta doesn’t publish a fixed time for these reviews. Check your email, including spam, and only trust messages that really come from Facebook or Meta. Sending several appeals does not move you up a queue and can make the record messier.</p>'),
  ('dont', 'What not to do',
   '<ul><li><strong>Don’t open a second profile.</strong> Meta may disable accounts created to get around earlier restrictions.</li><li><strong>Don’t admit to something you didn’t do</strong> just to sound cooperative.</li><li><strong>Don’t wait until the last week</strong> of the appeal window.</li><li><strong>Don’t share your login</strong> with anyone offering to “fix” it.</li></ul>')],
 [('How long do I have to appeal?', 'Facebook says suspended accounts generally have 180 days, but the time varies by region. Trust the date on your own notice and act early.'),
  ('Can Facebook disable an account without warning?', 'Yes. Facebook’s help page says Meta may disable an account without suspending it first for severe or time-sensitive violations.'),
  ('Will I find out which rule I broke?', 'Not always. The notice often names a general category. Account Status can show more, and it is worth checking if you can still reach it.'),
  ('Does it matter which device I appeal from?', 'Using a device and location you normally log in from helps, because Facebook weighs that signal.')],
 [FB_DISABLED, FB_HACKED, FB_HELP],
 [('/guides/facebook-account-disabled-appeal/', 'Facebook disabled account appeal'), ('/guides/facebook-disabled-appeal-rejected/', 'Facebook appeal rejected'), ('/guides/facebook-account-locked/', 'Facebook account locked'), ('/guides/hacked-facebook-account-recovery/', 'Hacked Facebook account recovery')],
 ['/guides/facebook-account-disabled-appeal/', '/guides/facebook-account-locked/', '/facebook-account-disabled-appeal-help/'],
 'Facebook disabled my account and I don’t know why. Can you help?', 'Facebook account disabled for no reason',
 ('No idea why?', 'Send us the notice', 'Share a screenshot of the notice and the date. We’ll help you work out whether it points to a hack, an identity issue or something else.'),
 ('Need a hand?', 'We’ll walk it through with you', 'Message us the account name and what you see. We use official tools only and never ask for your password or codes.'),
 pill='Facebook disabled'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(mk('facebook-disabled-no-appeal-option',
 'Facebook account disabled with no appeal option: what to do',
 'Facebook Disabled With No Appeal Option? What to Do',
 'No appeal button on your disabled Facebook notice? The usual reasons, what to try on another device, and the routes that may still be open.',
 'facebook', 'No appeal', 'Facebook',
 'check whether it is really a disabled notice, then try the latest app and a desktop browser. A missing button usually means the window has closed, the decision is final, or the screen is the wrong flow.',
 'Some Facebook notices show a disabled message with no “disagree” or review button at all. That usually has one of a few causes, and each has a different next step. Work through them in order before you decide the route is closed.',
 [('why', 'Why the button may be missing',
   '<ul><li><strong>The window has closed.</strong> Facebook’s help page says that if you don’t appeal within the allowed time, the account is permanently disabled and you can’t request another review.</li><li><strong>An appeal was already decided.</strong> An unsuccessful appeal leaves the account permanently disabled.</li><li><strong>Severe or time-sensitive violations.</strong> Meta may disable an account without a suspension step first.</li><li><strong>A different flow.</strong> “Your account is locked” and hacked-account screens work differently from a disabled notice.</li><li><strong>An app or display problem.</strong> An outdated app or a browser extension can hide the option.</li></ul>'),
  ('s1', 'Step 1: Make sure it is a disabled notice',
   '<p>Read the heading. <em>We suspended your account</em> and <em>We disabled your account</em> are the two states Facebook describes. A locked account asks you to confirm identity to unlock it, which is a separate process. A changed email or password you didn’t make points to a hack. Each has its own route, so identify the right one before anything else.</p>'),
  ('s2', 'Step 2: Try another device and the latest app',
   '<p>Update the Facebook app and log in again. Then try facebook.com in a desktop browser with extensions turned off, and try a device you have used to log in before. Many “no button” screens are display problems and the option appears on another device.</p>'),
  ('s3', 'Step 3: Check the email Facebook sent',
   '<p>Search the inbox and spam folder for messages from Facebook about the action. Some include a link to review or to download your information. Only use links from emails you can match to a message that appears in your Facebook notifications or security settings, and ignore any that ask for your password.</p>'),
  ('s4', 'Step 4: Use the hacked-account route if it applies',
   '<p>If someone else got into the account and the disabled notice followed their activity, go to facebook.com/hacked and follow the prompts. That flow is built for compromised accounts and can offer verification even when the disabled screen does not.</p>'),
  ('s5', 'Step 5: Look after Pages and ad accounts',
   '<p>If the profile is final, protect what it was connected to. Ask other admins to confirm they have full control of any Page, and check your business portfolio for people who can still manage ad accounts. Facebook says a Page with no one who has full control is deactivated.</p>'),
  ('dont', 'What not to do',
   '<ul><li><strong>Don’t use old appeal form links</strong> shared in videos and forums. Meta replaces forms often, and many old ones do nothing.</li><li><strong>Don’t pay anyone who says they have a secret appeal route.</strong></li><li><strong>Don’t make another account</strong> to get around the decision. Meta says it works to disable accounts created for that purpose.</li><li><strong>Don’t keep trying passwords.</strong> It can trigger more checkpoints.</li></ul>'),
  ('record', 'Keep a record',
   '<p>Note each device and browser you tried, the date, and exactly what appeared. If the option reappears later, appeal straight away. If you ask anyone for help, this record saves time and shows which routes you have already used.</p>')],
 [('Why is there no “disagree” button on my Facebook notice?', 'The appeal window may have closed, the decision may be final, or you may be looking at a locked or hacked-account screen. A missing button can also be an app problem, so try another device.'),
  ('Is there a form I can fill in instead?', 'Meta changes its forms often and old links get shared long after they stop working. Use Facebook’s current Help Center and the notice itself, not a link from a video or forum.'),
  ('Can Unbanz add an appeal button?', 'No. Nobody outside Meta can. We can help you work out which route applies and prepare a clean submission through the official process.'),
  ('What if the account was hacked first?', 'Use facebook.com/hacked. The hacked-account flow is separate from the disabled-account appeal.')],
 [FB_DISABLED, FB_HACKED, FB_RECOVERY, FB_HELP],
 [('/guides/facebook-account-disabled-appeal/', 'Facebook disabled account appeal'), ('/guides/facebook-disabled-appeal-rejected/', 'Facebook appeal rejected'), ('/guides/facebook-account-locked/', 'Facebook account locked'), ('/guides/instagram-disabled-no-appeal-button/', 'Instagram disabled, no appeal button')],
 ['/guides/facebook-account-disabled-appeal/', '/guides/facebook-account-locked/'],
 'my Facebook account is disabled and there is no appeal button. What can I do?', 'Facebook disabled no appeal option',
 ('No button?', 'Show us what the screen says', 'Send a screenshot of the notice. We’ll tell you which flow you are in and what is still open.'),
 ('Still stuck?', 'We’ll go through it with you', 'Message us the account name and what you tried. We use official tools only and never ask for your password or codes.'),
 pill='Facebook no appeal button'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(mk('hacker-changed-facebook-email-and-password',
 'Hacker changed your Facebook email and password: how to get back in',
 'Hacker Changed Facebook Email and Password: Get Back In',
 'Someone changed the email and password on your Facebook account? The undo link, facebook.com/hacked, identity checks, and how to lock it down afterwards.',
 'facebook', 'Hacked', 'Facebook',
 'look for Facebook’s email about the change and use its undo link if there is one, then go to facebook.com/hacked on a device you have used before. Secure your email and Pages straight away.',
 'When a hacker changes both the email and the password, the normal “forgot password” flow sends everything to them. Facebook has a separate route for this, and the first hour matters. Here is the order to work in.',
 [('s1', 'Step 1: Check the email Facebook sent about the change',
   '<p>When the email on an account is changed, Facebook sends a notice to the old address. Search your inbox and spam for it. If the message has a link to undo the change or secure the account, open it straight away on a device you trust, because the link may only work for a limited time. Make sure the message really is from Facebook by checking that it matches a notification in your own security settings if you can still reach them.</p>'),
  ('s2', 'Step 2: Go to facebook.com/hacked',
   '<p>Use a phone or computer you have logged in from before, on your usual network, and open <strong>facebook.com/hacked</strong>. Choose the option that says your account is compromised and follow the prompts. Facebook may ask for a name, an old password, a contact method you can reach, or an ID or video selfie. Answer carefully and use your real details.</p>'),
  ('s3', 'Step 3: Try to find the account directly',
   '<p>At facebook.com/login/identify you can search for your account by name, email or phone, then choose the option for no longer having access to those details. Add a new contact method you control when asked. If the hacker already removed your old contact details, the hacked-account flow is usually the better route.</p>'),
  ('s4', 'Step 4: Once you are back in, remove the attacker',
   '<ul><li>Change the password to a new one you have not used anywhere else.</li><li>Go to Settings, then Password and security, and log out of every device you do not recognise.</li><li>Remove any email address or phone number you did not add.</li><li>Review apps and websites with access to your account and remove ones you don’t know.</li><li>Turn on two-factor authentication with an authentication app, and save the recovery codes somewhere safe.</li></ul>'),
  ('s5', 'Step 5: Secure your Pages and ads',
   '<p>Hackers often add themselves as Page admins or take over a business portfolio. Check Page roles, the business portfolio, and ad accounts for people you do not know and for payment methods you did not add. If a card was used, tell your bank. A Page where the attacker removed other admins is covered in our guide to a hacked Page.</p>'),
  ('s6', 'Step 6: Warn your contacts',
   '<p>Post from another account or message close contacts to say not to click links or send money if they got something from your profile. Hackers often use a hijacked account to ask friends for money or to spread the same phishing link that caught you.</p>'),
  ('dont', 'What not to do',
   '<ul><li><strong>Don’t pay a “hacker recovery” service</strong> that contacts you first, or a person who offers to hack the hacker back.</li><li><strong>Don’t send ID or codes</strong> to anyone who messages you, including people claiming to be Facebook support.</li><li><strong>Don’t keep resetting the password</strong> from a new network every few minutes. It looks like the attacker.</li><li><strong>Don’t make a duplicate profile</strong> and tell friends the old one is gone. It makes the recovery and any impersonation reports harder.</li></ul>'),
  ('later', 'If the account was disabled afterwards',
   '<p>If the attacker posted scams and Facebook disabled the account, say in any appeal that someone else had access and give the approximate date. Our guide on disabled accounts covers the 180-day window and what to include.</p>')],
 [('Can Facebook give me my account back if the hacker changed everything?', 'It can offer recovery routes, such as facebook.com/hacked and identity checks. The outcome depends on Facebook’s checks, and nobody can promise it.'),
  ('How long do I have to use the undo link in Facebook’s email?', 'The link may only work for a limited time, so use it as soon as you see the email. If it has expired, move to facebook.com/hacked.'),
  ('Should I tell Facebook my friends were messaged?', 'Yes, mention it. It supports the report that the account was taken over, and tell your contacts not to click anything they received.'),
  ('Do I need to change my email password too?', 'Yes. If the email was breached, the attacker can reset Facebook again. Change that password, turn on two-factor authentication on the email, and check it for forwarding rules you did not create.')],
 [FB_HACKED, FB_IDENTIFY, FB_RECOVERY, FB_HELP],
 [('/guides/hacked-facebook-account-recovery/', 'Hacked Facebook account recovery'), ('/facebook-hacked-account-recovery-service/', 'Hacked Facebook recovery service'), ('/guides/facebook-page-hacked-admin-removed/', 'Facebook Page hacked, admin removed'), ('/guides/facebook-suspicious-login-active-sessions/', 'Suspicious Facebook logins')],
 ['/guides/hacked-facebook-account-recovery/', '/facebook-hacked-account-recovery-service/', '/guides/facebook-lost-access-to-email-or-phone/'],
 'a hacker changed the email and password on my Facebook account. Can you help?', 'Hacker changed my Facebook email and password',
 ('Locked out by a hacker?', 'Tell us what you can still reach', 'Share the email on the account, what you can still access, and the dates. We’ll point you to the right official route.'),
 ('Still locked out?', 'We’ll walk it through with you', 'Message us the account name and the screen you are on. We use official tools only and never ask for your password or codes.'),
 pill='Hacked Facebook'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(mk('instagram-appeal-sent-no-response',
 'Instagram appeal sent but no response: what to check',
 'Instagram Appeal Sent but No Response? What to Check',
 'Sent an Instagram appeal and heard nothing? Where replies show up, how to check your request, when to wait, and what not to do while you do.',
 'instagram', 'Appeal waiting', 'Instagram',
 'check the email on the account, including spam, and Support requests in the app. Wait before you try again, because repeat appeals add noise and don’t speed anything up.',
 'You submitted the appeal, saw the confirmation, and now nothing. Instagram doesn’t show a progress bar, so silence is hard to read. Here is where replies actually appear, what is normal, and how to avoid damaging a request that is still being looked at.',
 [('where', 'Where an Instagram reply appears',
   '<ul><li><strong>The email on the account.</strong> Instagram’s replies go to the email linked to the account, so check the spam and promotions folders too. If that email was changed by a hacker, the reply went to them.</li><li><strong>The login screen.</strong> Log in again. The notice can change when a decision has been made, for example to a message saying the account is back, or to a final decision.</li><li><strong>Support requests in the app.</strong> If you can log in, open Settings and activity, then Help, then Support requests, where your reports and appeals may be listed.</li><li><strong>Account Status.</strong> If you can still reach it, it shows strikes and restrictions on your account.</li></ul>'),
  ('verify', 'Check that an email is really from Instagram',
   '<p>Fake “Instagram support” emails are common after an appeal. If you can log in, Instagram’s security settings include a list of the recent emails it has sent you, covering roughly the last 14 days. Compare any message against that list. Real Instagram emails come from the mail.instagram.com domain. Never click a link in a message that asks for your password or a fee to “speed up” the appeal.</p>'),
  ('wait', 'How long is normal',
   '<p>Meta doesn’t publish a fixed time. Some people hear back within a day, others wait for days, and it can depend on the type of action and the checks involved. Follow any time shown on your own screen. Our guide on how long an Instagram appeal takes explains the pattern in more detail.</p>'
   '<p>A quiet few days is not a sign that the appeal was lost. A request submitted twice, or a second appeal started while the first is open, is more likely to cause confusion than to help.</p>'),
  ('s1', 'Step 1: Confirm the appeal actually went through',
   '<p>Did you see a confirmation screen, or an email saying Instagram received your request? If the app froze or you got an error at the end, the appeal may not have been submitted. In that situation, log in on the latest version of the app or at instagram.com and go through the review once more, finishing every step.</p>'),
  ('s2', 'Step 2: Check that you completed any verification step',
   '<p>Some appeals stay open until a verification step is finished, such as a video selfie or a code sent to the email or phone. If you closed the app halfway, go back to the notice and finish the step. Use your real details and good light for any selfie.</p>'),
  ('s3', 'Step 3: Make sure the contact details are still yours',
   '<p>If the email or phone on the account is no longer yours, replies will never reach you. If so, the account may have been taken over, and instagram.com/hacked is the better route. Say clearly that the contact details were changed and when.</p>'),
  ('s4', 'Step 4: Record what you did',
   '<p>Write down the date and time of the appeal, the device, and what the screen said. If you later ask anyone for help, this record shows what was sent and avoids duplicate requests.</p>'),
  ('dont', 'What not to do',
   '<ul><li><strong>Don’t send repeat appeals every few hours.</strong> Extra requests make the history noisier without moving anything forward.</li><li><strong>Don’t create a new account</strong> to chase the old one. Meta says it may disable accounts created to get around a restriction.</li><li><strong>Don’t pay anyone who claims to be inside Instagram</strong> and promises to push the appeal. No outside party can.</li><li><strong>Don’t share your password or codes</strong> with a person who offers to check the status for you.</li></ul>')],
 [('How long should I wait for an Instagram appeal reply?', 'Instagram doesn’t publish a fixed time. Follow any time shown on your own screen, check your email and spam, and avoid sending a second appeal while the first is open.'),
  ('Can I check the status of my Instagram appeal?', 'There is no tracker. You can log in to see if the notice changed, check the email on the account, and look in Support requests in the app if you can reach it.'),
  ('What if the reply went to an old email?', 'If the email on the account is no longer yours, replies won’t reach you. Use instagram.com/hacked and explain that the contact details were changed.'),
  ('Does contacting Instagram again help?', 'Not usually. One complete appeal is better than several. If a different route applies, such as the hacked-account flow, use that instead of repeating the same one.')],
 [IG_DISABLED, IG_APPEAL, IG_HACKED, IG_HELP],
 [('/how-long-does-an-instagram-appeal-take/', 'How long an Instagram appeal takes'), ('/guides/instagram-check-account-status/', 'Check your Instagram account status'), ('/instagram-appeal-denied/', 'Instagram appeal denied'), ('/guides/instagram-account-suspended-appeal/', 'Instagram suspended appeal')],
 ['/how-long-does-an-instagram-appeal-take/', '/instagram-appeal-denied/', '/guides/instagram-account-suspended-appeal/'],
 'I sent my Instagram appeal days ago and heard nothing. Can you check what is going on?', 'Instagram appeal no response',
 ('Heard nothing?', 'Tell us when you appealed', 'Send the date, the notice and the email on the account. We’ll tell you whether it is normal to wait or whether another route fits.'),
 ('Still waiting?', 'We’ll go through it with you', 'Message us your username and what you see. We use official tools only and never ask for your password or codes.'),
 pill='Instagram appeal waiting'))
